tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28932:     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork's guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design's 'is B well-defined at reset' MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule's own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork's own comment says 'only bit 0 is implemented so far', an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat's 'not a functional defect' OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs > len' termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28942:     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork's guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design's 'is B well-defined at reset' MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule's own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork's own comment says 'only bit 0 is implemented so far', an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat's 'not a functional defect' OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs > len' termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28952:     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork's guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design's 'is B well-defined at reset' MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule's own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork's own comment says 'only bit 0 is implemented so far', an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat's 'not a functional defect' OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs > len' termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28959:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` §7i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28969:   "note": "*** A SURVIVAL ORACLE THAT A WRONG `exit(0)` SATISFIES IS NOT A SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  Found by the codex seat while answering `sgideprimop`'s pass-1 brief, in shipped material it was not asked about.  `regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py` pins host survival with `row(..., how, \"exit0\", ...)` at **NINE sites across FIVE probe files**, derived rather than transcribed with `grep -rn 'how, \\\"exit0\\\"' regress/*.py`:  `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4 (`lo_alive`, `lo_alive2`, `hi_alive`, `hi_rd_alive`), `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1.  *codex named three, in the one file it had been given; the class is three times that and spans devices it was never shown -- which is why the row is filed as a CLASS and the deriver is written down instead of the number.*  The oracle reads the process's exit status after the harness sends `quit`.  **An emulator that calls `exit(0)` on the guest access under test ALSO exits 0**, so the row cannot distinguish *the host survived and was then asked to quit* from *the host died quietly at exactly the moment the row exists to observe*.  **THE REPAIR codex NAMES:** assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally followed by a post-trigger command whose result proves the emulator was still answering.  The probe already captures `alive` -- the rows simply do not use it.  *** RANKED AS COVERAGE DEBT, NOT AN L2, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE DOCUMENTED ONE. ***  These rows are in a SHIPPED detector that sits above the vacuity floor: it fails on the pre-fix build, on a verbatim reinstatement (14/25, eleven reds) and on a null medium.  The stopping rule files findings against shipped material and fixes only MEASURED FALSE PASSES and WRONG RECORDS.  **No mutant has been built that exploits this**, so it is a hypothetical edit in an uncovered cell -- filed, ranked below live defects in the same file.  *What would promote it to L2: a measured mutant that calls `exit(0)` on the declined path and sweeps the detector clean.  That is one build and is the natural first job for whoever takes this row.*  **CHECK THE CLASS BEFORE FIXING THE INSTANCE** -- `grep -rn 'exit0' regress/*.py` across the tree, since the idiom will have been copied; this project has twice corrected an instance and left its siblings standing.  *** THE COUNT IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 9, AND THE POPULATION IS 13.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by the adjudicating seat, which could not reproduce my figure by any method. ***  **THE DERIVER, and it is the fix as well as the count:** `grep -rn '\"exit0\"' regress/*.py`, counting `row(...)` want-sites -- **13 across 5 files**: `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 5, `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4, `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1 (that one INSIDE A LOOP, so its runtime rows exceed its textual site).  **My grep was `'how, \"exit0\"'` -- anchored on the VARIABLE NAME rather than the ORACLE VALUE -- and `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` uses `how1`, `how2`, `how3`, `how4`.** Four sites invisible to a pattern that looked precise.  *A row filed to adjudicate oracle blindness shipped with a count produced by a blind pattern; that is the frozen-number rule arriving inside the filing that names it.*  *** THE RANKING SURVIVES, AND THE SEAT GAVE IT A MEASURED REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THE BLINDNESS IS PER-ROW, NOT PER-DETECTOR. ***  `lo_sent` reads `printed(t, \"v0\") if alive else \"died\"`, so it reds on ANY death whatever the exit code -- verified by the seat, which built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found it KILLED by the read-side sentinel rows even though all four `*_alive` rows passed it.  **So no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole (not L2), and no record is made false where a session-mate positive row exists (not L3).**  Coverage debt, filed, med -- confirmed, on evidence rather than on the stopping rule alone.  **THE FIXING ROUND'S FIRST JOB IS THEREFORE A NARROWER CENSUS: which of the 13 lack a death-proof SESSION-MATE?**  Those are the live ones; the rest are redundant belt-and-braces and can be repaired cheaply or left.  *That is a smaller and better-aimed job than \"repair 13 rows\".*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l = 24 on 2026-08-31\n\n*** THE COUNT WENT STALE WITHIN HOURS OF BEING FILED, BY THE FILER'S OWN EDIT. ***  Filed 2026-08-31 as THIRTEEN; derived the SAME DAY as TWENTY-FOUR, of which sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py alone holds ELEVEN because #499 pass 4 added seven.  That is this project's documented pattern exactly -- the rounds that consult a number are the rounds that change it -- so the title now carries the DERIVER and no literal.  (Demoted: 13, filed 2026-08-31, never a current claim.)  Per-file on that date: sgi_re_unmapped 11, gdrom_toclen 5, sgi_de_width 4, gdrom_decline 2, gdrom_default 1, sgi_re_width 1.\n\nAND THE CLASS BEARS ON #499's OWN PASS-4 ROWS.  Of the twelve rows pass 4 added, five are SURVIVAL-ONLY with an `exit0` want.  The READ side is covered -- `clip rd sent` reads `died` when the host is gone.  The WRITE side has NO post-death positive observable: surv wants exit0 and is satisfied, decl already counted its message before the exit.  A WRITE-SIDE-ONLY exit(0) is therefore PREDICTED to escape all 48 rows.  That is clause (d) shaped -- guard kept, its repair (falling through to the common tail) removed -- so it is NOT filed on my reading; it is QUEUED AS MUTANT E0W and will be measured.  Floor clause (b) is unaffected and measured green: a verbatim exit(1) reinstatement scores 18/48.\n\nAND A SECOND SITE FOUND THE SAME DAY BY THE lcabadval SEAT, which makes this a CLASS rather than a probe-local habit: on `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` (dev_lca.c) are bare fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert or deassert kills the host with the SAME exit status as the value guards under test.  Any lcabadval row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM, never the exit status -- an exit-status oracle there cannot tell the defect from the confound.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29012:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** THE WITNESS HAD A ROW THAT COULD NOT FAIL, AND A PASS-2 REVIEW OF THE ROUND FOUND IT.\nREPAIRED AND CONTROLLED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe rows tested `\"IOC_CONF: 0x100000000\" in text` -- a SUBSTRING -- and manufactured the displayed\n`got` from the boolean.  codex: *\"Append one literal 0 before the diagnostic's newline.  The message\nbecomes 0x1000000000, yet `kill width` still reports a green 0x100000000.\"*  `zero` had the mirror\nflaw (`0x0abc` contains `0x0`).\n\n> **That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape INSIDE THE WITNESS FOR THE DEFECT IT EXISTS TO PIN** --\n> and it survived my own review, a design review, and the run.  It was found only by asking a seat\n> what edit keeps the witness green.\n\n**REPAIRED** to parse the number and compare integers, reporting the value actually found.\n**CONTROLLED in both directions:**\n\n    fixed build, repaired oracle          6/6      kill width 0x100000000\n    codex's escape (TRAILING 0)           5/6      kill width 0x1000000000  RED\n\n*** AND MY FIRST CONTROL TESTED THE WRONG MUTANT, which is worth recording because it would have\nbeen a false proof. ***  I applied the extra `0` BEFORE the value (`0x0%\" PRIx64`), giving\n`0x0100000000` -- **numerically identical to `0x100000000`**, so the row passed correctly and proved\nnothing.  That is the carrier's own *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT* rule, case 1: the mutant\nis mis-built.  **The leading-zero form is a genuine EQUIVALENT mutant** and is recorded as such\nrather than as an escape.  The trailing-zero form is codex's, and it reds.\n\n*Artifacts: `probe_fix_newora_20260907.out`, `probe_mutant_trailzero_20260907.out`, and the\nmis-built `probe_mutant_extrazero_20260907.out` kept as the record of the equivalence.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/devices/dev_lca.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/devices/dev_lca.c\n                                   contains the `#506' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29593:     "note": "PACKET-FED (deepseek-v4-pro:cloud).  First firing HTTP 429 (two panels fired 10 s apart -- eight concurrent cloud POSTs); re-fired once, 2.5 KB.  NOT RATIFIED on three points, two accepted as wording L4s in the brief: premise 2 over-generalises (reads crossing the 0x40 boundary are zero-filled, not served -- true; irrelevant to byte offsets); 'every exit path is return 1' should read 'every RETURN path' (the exit(1)s are exits too); the BACKED ACCEPT warrant is not a valid addition-licence warrant (agrees with the panel)."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29988:   "state_note": "\n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] Witness built (rung 3) before any edit: HEAD 0/9 (every trap-like class stopped the machine), fix 9/9 (16:16 build).  Pre-table check PRETABLE_m88kexc.out written before table 1.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / research] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 1 panel_20260905_143211: Opus (MEASURED: the one-site form halts at the tail default; supervisor tb1 naming 7 halts HEAD -- BOUNDS reachable), kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; codex GPT-6 Astra REFUSED twice (content filter); glm BURNED (0-byte response, 117 KB thinking; mini packet answered); Fable WALLED (session limit) and re-fired at pass 2; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass1/.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 2 panel_20260905_212630: codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax, Opus (measuring: four 10/10 escapes), Fable (review: delay-slot arm wrong; level observable) answered; glm BURNED then answered the mini packet ollama_glm_mini.txt; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Every seat: the DIFF is right; the DETECTOR needed repairs -- made (v3, 22 rows) and re-measured (table 3, 14 mutants: 13 killed, M_TRAPONLY equivalent).  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass2_panel_20260905_212630/, opus_p2.md, fable_p2.md.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS (F1..F24, D1..D3; brief_m88kexc_adj.md) fired ~23:10 to all nine (agy not fired): results land in the closure entry.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS landed (pass3_panel_20260905_220902): codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; glm BURNED again; Opus and Fable Agent seats DIED on an Anthropic session limit (429) and are re-fired on pass 4; agy not fired.  grok/kimi CLOSE; codex/deepseek/minimax ONE MORE REPAIR -> v4: row indclr (IND never started clear -- the carry shape again), HALT classifier requires the stopped state + the named vector (+ pc == 0x40 for sfu1h), LEVEL contract narrowed to the ERROR/WARNING regression, sfu1h's channel restated (nested SFU1 halts at the fallback; with it off, re-routed to ERROR); mutants M_NOIND, M_NOSTOP; table 4 re-measures the carried-forward table-1 shapes.  Records: census (11 bypass / 10 delivered / vector 0 RESET-stop / 117 fallback), table-1 tally 11+2, M_TAILOFF equivalent, gate 2/22->3/22, PRETABLE correction appended, three phantom rows filed (m88kexctaildefault, m88ktbnd, m88kmxmkill).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-5 REPAIR (probe v5, 29 fault rows, md5 96ab47a0 shipped / c83b1f38 frozen -- docstring-only diff): rows umisalign, uovf, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl; classifier keeps one `stopped` observation.  TABLE 5 (killtable5/, PRETABLE5 written 08:47 before it): BASE 29/29; M_OVFSUPONLY 28/29 (uovf), M_MISSUPONLY 28/29 (umisalign), M_SFRZMIS 25/29 (the four err* rows), M_SFRZNOOVF/NODIV0/NOPRIV/NOUNIMPL 28/29 each (one row each, as predicted), M_NOSFD1 1/29 (27 bad=psr + sfu1h STOP; resid50 the survivor).  HEAD 2/29 (probe_v5_head.out: usertb DELIVER, resid50 HALT); fix 29/29 (probe_v5_fix_final.out).  Comment corrections (Table 6-3 attribution; the tb1-naming-10 frozen case) propagated to all trees, rebuilt, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH x3 (rebuild_fix2.log).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR (one diff, two classes found by pass 5): probe v6 adds resid127 (the fallback window's FAR bound; table 6 = killtable6/: M_FALLBACK127 29/29 on v5 -> 29/30 on v6; M_FALLBACK51 28/29 on v5 (sfu1h saw it) -> 28/30; M_FALLBACKLE 28/29 -> 29/30 control); probe v7 adds trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 with a harness field `handler_at` (trapfrozen8 plants its handler at the OVF entry so the frozen trap naming DIV0 is observable), class 10 disclosed as observationally identical; table 7 = killtable7/: M_SFRZTRAP4/5/6/8/9 30/30 on v6 -> 34/35 each (own row), M_SFRZTRAP 29/35.  HEAD with v7: 3/35 (usertb DELIVER, resid50 and resid127 HALT); fix 35/35.  PRETABLE6/7 written before their tables; both PRETABLE corrections were APPENDED to the same file after the table and refreshed its mtime -- stated in the record; corrections go to separate files from now on.  Gate pins 35/35 + resid127 HALT + six trapfrozen DELIVER.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR part 3 (probe v8, 36 rows, md5 e550e3fe): row usfu1 for kimi's measured M_FBSUPONLY (fallback conditioned on MODE: 35/35 on v7 -> 35/36 on v8); M_SFRZTRAP4 re-measured 35/36.  HEAD with v8: 4/36; fix 36/36.  Gate pins 36/36 + usfu1 HALT.  Pass 6 fired ~10:30 to the eight scriptable transports (Opus/Fable walled).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v9+v10, 41 rows, md5 ee2c78c2 = shipped): rows trapfrozen10, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, trapfrozen128 and the refatal channel (the re-route's diagnostic pinned present on err* rows, absent on trapfrozen* rows).  TABLE 10 (killtable10/, PRETABLE10 before it, untouched; table 9 folded): M_ERRORFRZOFF, M_SFRZTRAP10, M_FB_LO, M_FBIND, M_FBNOC, M_SFRZTRAPUSER each 36/36 on the frozen v8 (the escapes measured) and 40/41 on v10, killed by exactly the predicted row (M_SFRZTRAP10 via bad=refatal on trapfrozen10).  BASE 41/41; HEAD 7/41 (usertb DELIVER; six HALT rows); fix 41/41.  Also repaired this pass: three gate check lines with a literal backslash-n between arguments (codex, pass 6) -- would have failed on correct output.  Gate pins 41/41 + the seven HALT rows + eight frozen-trap rows + refatal absent.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v10 -> v14, 46 fault rows; shipped v15 md5 eb11ab7c, identical to frozen v14 outside the module docstring, AST-compared).  Rows added: trapfrozen450 / trapfrozen496 (codex: switch 1's last two deliverable labels), trapfrozen511 (kimi: the exemption's far edge, tb1's 9-bit field), errind / errc (grok: the re-route x {IND, C} -- the re-route predicate reads PSR, so a PSR-bit conjunct there is clause (d), unlike the switch-1 arms).  TABLE 11 (killtable11/, PRETABLE11 11:49 + addendum 2 11:55 + addendum 3 12:16, all before the 12:17 start; frozen v14 md5 99eb9c00; each mutant ALSO scored on the frozen v10): M_SFRZTRAP450 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAP496 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAPHI 41/41 -> 43/46; M_ERRORFRZON 40/41 -> 45/46 (kimi's sweep CLAIM refuted by measurement, as the existing errortb/bounds rows predicted); M_MISNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc HALT: the disclosed {MISALIGNED} x {C} representative is killed as a side effect of the re-route rows -- disclosure narrowed in v15's docstring); M_RRIND 41/41 -> 45/46 (errind); M_RRNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc).  Six measured sweeps of v10, each killed by the row the PRETABLE named.  BASE 46/46 before and after; clone cmp-clean, sentinel clear.  A MIS-CUT ROW was caught by the driver's own BASE gate (killtable11_attempt1_v13/): v13's errc wanted PSR 0x8000000b and exception entry leaves C alone, so BASE read 45/46 bad=psr before any mutant and the driver aborted -- PSR_WANT repaired in the instrument only (v14), no product code, no mutant re-aimed.  HEAD 7/46 (probe_v14_head.out, re-run on the HEAD binary with the final instrument); fix 46/46 (probe_v14_fix_final.out).  Gate pins 46/46, err(...|ind|c) 6, trapfrozen(...|511)? 11.  Pass 8 packet drafted (brief_m88kexc_p8.DRAFT.md).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS 8 FIRED (_scratchpad/panel_20260906_150800_m88k_p8/, brief = the FROZEN artifact copy regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/brief_m88kexc_p8.md, per the new template section on citing frozen copies).  Seat state MEASURED at launch, not assumed: opus5 ALIVE and fired first (it is the only measuring-seat review this round gets before it commits, since fable5 is walled); codex, kimi and grok fired and running -- the brief is 31,166 b, above the 30,000 argv cap, so codex and kimi got the POINTER form and grok is immune (--prompt-file).  *** THE THREE OLLAMA CLOUD SEATS ARE DOWN ON AN ACCOUNT-LEVEL QUOTA, NOT A TRANSIENT RATE LIMIT, AND THE SEAT FILES COULD NOT SAY SO. ***  Two firings forty minutes apart returned `OLLAMA_SEAT_ERROR: <HTTPError 429: 'Too Many Requests'>` and nothing else, which is indistinguishable from a five-minute wait; a direct POST to the same endpoint carried the reason in the response BODY -- a WEEKLY account usage limit.  Owner states it resets in about four hours from ~15:10 (so ~19:10).  panel_ollama.py now reads the HTTPError's body and appends it, CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS on the live wall: the fixed form prints the weekly-limit text, the pre-fix form from HEAD prints the bare status.  Pass 8's Ollama third fires when the quota resets; until then the stage is 6/9 and the three are recorded DOWN by name, never as agreement.  agy remains DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner ruling, reserved for measurement).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **TABLE 12 -- THE DISCRIMINATING TABLE.  THE PASS-8 PANEL SPLIT IS SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, AND codex AND grok ARE RIGHT.**  (regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/killtable12/ with its driver log; PRETABLE12 written 15:58:33 and the first BASE at 16:00:48, so the order proof is the file's own mtime; the frozen instrument is the SHIPPED probe v15, md5 eb11ab7c -- which also closes the records gap kimi named, since the gate runs the shipped file and earlier tables measured a frozen copy of an older revision.)  | mutant | on frozen v10 | on shipped v15 | red rows |  M_RRTRAPIND `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & IND))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  M_RRTRAPC `if (!is_trap || (PSR & C))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  CTL_RRTRAPINV `if (!is_trap || (PSR & IND))` -- THE POLARITY TWIN | 33/41 | **35/46** | ELEVEN: trapfrozen, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 450, 496, 511, 128 all STOP, and trapfrozen10 BADREGS bad=refatal |  **THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES THE SWEEPS MEAN SOMETHING.**  It is the same edit with one token changed -- the sense of the bit test -- and it reddens exactly the eleven frozen-trap rows, on both channels the PRETABLE named (the entry pc moves to 0x50, so the breakpoint is never reached; and trapfrozen10 additionally shows the re-route's diagnostic where REFATAL_EXPECT says it must not appear).  So the find-text landed, the probe CAN see this site, and the two 46/46 results are genuine blindness rather than a mis-built mutant -- the three explanations a non-dying mutant has, reduced to one.  BASE 46/46 before and 46/46 after; HEAD 7/46 with the same instrument; the rebuilt build/ 46/46; clone cmp-clean against SEC with no sentinel.  All five PRETABLE12 predictions held, including the control's.  **WHAT IT SETTLES.**  kimi's `killable-and-undisclosed survivors: ZERO' is refuted at the RE-ROUTE as it already was at the switch-1 arms -- and the reason is visible in kimi's own enumeration, which wrote the re-route family as `{label trap x 11}': the eleven rows cover eleven LABELS in ONE status state, and every one of them arrives with PSR 0x8000000b, so no row varies the trap side's status at all.  Counting labels is not covering the axis.  **WHAT IS NOW OWED.**  Three consecutive passes have found this class at three sites -- the fallback predicate (pass 6), the re-route's non-trap side (pass 7), and now the re-route's TRAP side plus the switch-1 arms (pass 8, measured here and by opus5) -- so the class-closure amendment applies and the repair must close the enumerated space in ONE diff rather than buy another cell.  opus5's five rows close the arms; these two cells close the re-route's trap side.  L2, in-round.\n\n*** THE PASS-5 PANEL WAS CITED AT A DURABLE PATH THAT DID NOT EXIST; THE EVIDENCE WAS SITTING IN\n`_scratchpad/`, WHICH IS NEVER COMMITTED.  Found and fixed 2026-09-08. ***\n\nFive seat entries cite `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass5_panel_20260906_090810/<seat>.txt`.\n**That directory did not exist.**  The panel was at `_scratchpad/panel_20260906_090810/` -- the\nvolatile path -- so the citations were unverifiable and the answers were one cleanup from gone.\n\n**COPIED to the cited path**, and all five now resolve:\n\n    codex.txt          1,299,047 b      grok.txt              5,213 b\n    ollama_glm.txt         3,704 b      ollama_minimax.txt    7,559 b\n    ollama_deepseek.txt    1,297 b      SEATS.txt               829 b\n\n*** AND A SIXTH SEAT ANSWERED THAT THE LEDGER NEVER CITED: `kimi.txt`, 312,574 BYTES. ***  It is\nrecorded in no entry on this row.  **That is an UNREAD REVIEW -- not a seat failure and not\nagreement -- and this project has found real defects in one four times.**  Preserved with the rest;\n**reading it is an outstanding obligation on this row**, not discharged here.\n\n**A SIZE NOTE, recorded rather than acted on unilaterally: `codex.txt` is 1.3 MB.**  The carrier\nrecords that a codex research run here has exceeded 1 MB, and `adjudicate.sh` takes only the TAIL\nfor that reason.  **I did NOT truncate it**: the ledger cites this file as a seat's evidence, and\ntruncating cited evidence to save space converts a verifiable citation into a modified artifact --\nwhich is worse than a large file.  If the repository size matters more than the citation, that is\nan owner decision and this note is where it should start.\n\n> **THE GENERAL FINDING, which is why this was worth an audit rather than a fix: the volatile-path\n> rule was OBEYED for the round's own logs and MISSED for its panel.**  A round that carefully\n> copies its kill-table output to `regress/artifacts/` can still leave the panel that reviewed it in\n> `_scratchpad/`, because the panel feels like process rather than evidence.  **It is evidence: the\n> ledger cites it by path.**\n\n*Found by an EXISTENCE audit over every artifact path in the ledger's witness and `ev` fields --\n71 witness citations, all resolving, and 181 `ev` paths of which these five were the only misses.\nAn existence check cannot false-positive the way a count or a proximity grep can, which is why it is\nworth running and the stale-count audit was declined.*\n\n*** THE AUDIT'S FIRST RUN REPORTED EIGHT ADDITIONAL MISSES AND EVERY ONE WAS ITS OWN BUG. ***  Bare\nfilenames (`check_witness.py`, `gate_arm.sh`, `precommit_check.sh` ...) were joined to the SEC root\nwhen they live in `tools/pipeline/` and `regress/`, and one citation carried a `GXEMUL-SEC/` prefix\nthat the join then doubled.  **With the resolver fixed: 71 of 71.**  *Recorded because reporting\nthose eight as ledger defects would have been exactly the wrong-apparatus failure this project keeps\ncatching -- and the tell was that they were all HARNESS files, which is a suspicious population.*",
