entries: [{"seat":"glm","phase":"assess","d":"OK","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"CONFIRMED","outcome":"UPHELD","grader":"mainloop -- cross-family","ev":"_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346","outcome_note":"[PREMISE-REFUTED-AND-A-CONTRADICTION-IN-THE-BRIEF]  *** THE ROUND\u0027S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row\u0027s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed","note":"*** THE ROUND\u0027S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row\u0027s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed decode or does the emulator not model them?  Both were asserted. ***  The source settles it against me: base/mask/tlb_enable are written and read back and nothing else; only `ioc_conf` is consumed (`bus = d-\u003eioc_conf \u0026 1`).  **That splits the fix design** -- three arms are pure storage where a decline may keep the store, and the fourth is not.  Also the sharpest design point of the panel: for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the MAXIMALLY ORDINARY write, so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one.  Named `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` as the single fact that would close the census, which is exactly the right move for a packet-fed seat."},{"seat":"deepseek","phase":"assess","d":"OK","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"CONFIRMED","outcome":"UPHELD","grader":"mainloop -- cross-family","ev":"_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346","outcome_note":"[CENSUS-CORRECTED-REACHABILITY-UNSETTLEABLE]  Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would sett","note":"Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would settle whether these are hardware-legal values or merely the ones this emulator was developed against.  There is no such document in `_scratchpad/`."},{"seat":"minimax","phase":"assess","d":"OK","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"CONFIRMED","outcome":"UPHELD","grader":"mainloop -- cross-family","ev":"_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346","outcome_note":"[FOUR-ARMS-FOUR-SETTLING-FACTS]  Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat.","note":"Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat."},{"seat":"codex","phase":"assess","d":"N/A","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"UNKNOWN","outcome":"HELD","grader":"mainloop","ev":"_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346","outcome_note":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex, grok, kimi, opus5, fable5]  the packet-fed seats settled the census and the consumption question; the tree questions remain.","note":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex, grok, kimi, opus5, fable5]  Three packet-fed seats answered and settled what a packet can settle -- the accept-set census, and (once I checked it) which fields are consumed.  The rest needs the tree."},{"seat":"opus5","phase":"research","d":"N/A","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"MANUAL","outcome":"ANSWERED","grader":"mainloop -- every claim re-verified by reading","ev":"agent seat, read-only; claims re-checked against dev_lca.c by the main loop","outcome_note":"RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none\u0027 would have meant `did not think to ask\u0027. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null\u0027; machine_alpha.c\u0027s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,\"lca\").  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279\u0027s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh\u0027s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)\u0027 = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -\u003e memory_rw.\n\n*** THE CONFOUND, and it is why an exit-status oracle here is worthless -- VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c: `lca_interrupt_assert\u0027 and `lca_interrupt_deassert\u0027 are BARE fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert OR deassert ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test. ***  Every row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM.  The two tick candidates are quiescent at a cold prompt (8253 constructed with in_use=0; ns16550 asserts only with com_ier bits set), so it is controllable -- do not touch the ISA window.","note":"RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none\u0027 would have meant `did not think to ask\u0027. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null\u0027; machine_alpha.c\u0027s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,\"lca\").  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279\u0027s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh\u0027s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)\u0027 = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -\u003e memory_rw.\n\n*** THE CONFOUND, and it is why an exit-status oracle here is worthless -- VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c: `lca_interrupt_assert\u0027 and `lca_interrupt_deassert\u0027 are BARE fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert OR deassert ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test. ***  Every row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM.  The two tick candidates are quiescent at a cold prompt (8253 constructed with in_use=0; ns16550 asserts only with com_ier bits set), so it is controllable -- do not touch the ISA window."},{"seat":"fable5","phase":"research","d":"N/A","date":"2026-08-31","grade":"UNKNOWN","outcome":"HELD","grader":"mainloop","ev":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]","outcome_note":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row\u0027s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm\u0027s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth\u0027s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger\u0027s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.","note":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row\u0027s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm\u0027s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth\u0027s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger\u0027s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.  QUESTION (added 2026-09-08, after codex settled the disposition and left the hardware claim open): The DISPOSITION is now settled by codex against #447\u0027s actual implementation: decline the out-of-set write and PRESERVE the previous value (the queue\u0027s option B), with the store moved BELOW the guard -- because today the store runs FIRST (dev_lca.c:233 vs the guard at :236), so merely deleting the exit(1) leaves the bad value STORED and guest-readable.  What is NOT settled, and is this row\u0027s research question: **what does the real 21066 LCA do with an out-of-set value in these six registers?**  We have NO LCA datasheet on this machine -- `_scratchpad/*.pdf` holds none -- and the only primary source is the OpenBSD 2.2 driver (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c`), which shows what a GUEST writes and not what the CHIP accepts.  So: (a) is decline-and-preserve defensible as MODELLING, or is it merely the safest thing to do in ignorance -- and should the round SAY the latter rather than imply the former?  (b) The guard\u0027s accepted sets are themselves unverified: W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 REJECT their own reset value of 0, and the header names 0 as IOC_W_MASK_1M, which suggests 0 is a legal encoding the whitelist omits.  **Is the correct round actually to widen or delete the whitelists rather than to change what happens when they fail?**","hold_question":"The DISPOSITION is now settled by codex against #447\u0027s actual implementation: decline the out-of-set write and PRESERVE the previous value (the queue\u0027s option B), with the store moved BELOW the guard -- because today the store runs FIRST (dev_lca.c:233 vs the guard at :236), so merely deleting the exit(1) leaves the bad value STORED and guest-readable.  What is NOT settled, and is this row\u0027s research question: **what does the real 21066 LCA do with an out-of-set value in these six registers?**  We have NO LCA datasheet on this machine -- `_scratchpad/*.pdf` holds none -- and the only primary source is the OpenBSD 2.2 driver (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c`), which shows what a GUEST writes and not what the CHIP accepts.  So: (a) is decline-and-preserve defensible as MODELLING, or is it merely the safest thing to do in ignorance -- and should the round SAY the latter rather than imply the former?  (b) The guard\u0027s accepted sets are themselves unverified: W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 REJECT their own reset value of 0, and the header names 0 as IOC_W_MASK_1M, which suggests 0 is a legal encoding the whitelist omits.  **Is the correct round actually to widen or delete the whitelists rather than to change what happens when they fail?**"},{"seat":"codex","phase":"assess","date":"2026-09-06","note":"GPT-6 Astra, max effort, on a purpose-written assess brief (answer preserved at _scratchpad/codex_lcabadval/ANSWER_astra_20260906.txt; refused the first packet on its content filter and answered the neutralised one).  **IT CORRECTED THE ROW\u0027S CENSUS AND MY BRIEF\u0027S, AND I VERIFIED THE SHARPEST CORRECTION BY OPENING THE FILE.**  Comment-aware counting gives **12 textual exits, 11 LIVE**: six value guards, two interrupt, one nonzero-bus, one ISA-range, one IOC-default.  **The `dev \u003e= 21\u0027 guard my brief listed as a live range kill is COMMENTED OUT** -- dev_lca.c has `/*  fatal(...); exit(1);  */` inside it and assigns `dev = 0;` instead.  Verified.  So the brief asked about a dead guard, and the premise-audit opener is what surfaced it.  Also: exactly THREE guards accept a single value (not four), and FOUR diagnostics carry the phrase `from the only implemented\u0027.  **IT ALSO REFUTED THE CONFOUND AS I STATED IT**, which changes the witness design rather than the defect: `Any interrupt overstates the confound\u0027 -- bus_isa.c (anchor `old_isa_assert == new_isa_assert\u0027) filters unchanged masked state, so only callbacks that actually PROPAGATE terminate.  The oracle still cannot rest on exit status, but the interference is narrower and testable.  Its witness specification: drive the named IOC cases through the real memory decode with native quadword accesses, and require same-decode liveness, a device-specific signature beyond an ordinary write/read echo, independent value and width recording, state preservation, and latch repetition plus instance controls -- with the oracle validated against separate interrupt-diagnostic fixtures.  It found NO Alpha or LCA regression probe anywhere in the tree, which is why this file has no coverage at all."},{"date":"2026-09-06","phase":"research","seat":"codex","note":"DESIGN PASS.  VERDICT: severity order = the two INTERRUPT STUBS first, provisionally (firmware execution order unproved); PREMISE 3 IS WRONG.  *** IT ADDED A SEVENTH MEMBER TO THE DE-FATALISATION OPTION SET: IMPLEMENT. ***  All six existing members presume a refusal decision; for an unimplemented PATH none applies -- \u0027declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete\u0027.  *** AND IT FOUND THE FAULT MEMBER IS UNAVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE: memory_rw.c dispatches an exception on a failed device access for MIPS and M88K with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT ***, so the dev_unreadable.c idiom does not transfer and any round reaching for FAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  Corrected three of my five premises: (2) the commented-out dev\u003e=21 kill is NOT a usable precedent -- its live `dev = 0\u0027 ALIASES \u0027no selected device\u0027 to device zero, safe today only because slot 0 is empty; (3) W_BASE0 accepts TWO values, so only three window guards are single-value; (5) \u0027firmware is the only writer\u0027 is unsupported, since guest kernel stores use the same MMIO path and interrupt callbacks arise from device events.  Also flagged that the Alpha callbacks only set irq_asserted and the execution loop never consumes it, so implementing the stubs would not by itself deliver an interrupt.  Answer preserved at regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt"},{"seat":"codex","phase":"research","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"CONFIRMED","outcome":"qualified-yes","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_fixcheck_20260907.txt","note":"CODEX FIX-CHECK on the six-arm proposal, 2026-09-07.  VERDICT: **Qualified yes** -- \"the six stores and your consumer analysis check out ... the sibling precedent does not establish hardware correctness or close the remaining termination paths.\"  Artifact: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_fixcheck_20260907.txt.  Brief: _scratchpad/brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md -- a CHECK-MY-CONSTRUCTION brief, with the seat explicitly told not to design a probe or choose register values (the C5 scoping rule).\n\nDENOMINATOR GIVEN, which is what makes this a check rather than an opinion: all 10 cases plus the `default:\u0027 arm of DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc); all six guarded arms; both translated-base siblings; and all nine field-read sites across the eight fields -- eight readbacks and ONE functional consumer.\n\n**Q1 CONFIRMED WITH A TABLE.**  The store really is unconditional and prior to the guard in ALL SIX arms -- readback line, store line and guard line tabulated per arm.  My generalisation from one arm holds.\n\n**Q2 CONFIRMED, WITH A SHARPER READING THAN MINE.**  Five fields are pure storage.  `ioc_conf\u0027 has exactly one additional consumer, `bus = d-\u003eioc_conf \u0026 1\u0027 at dev_lca.c:121.  But the nuance matters and my brief blurred it: *extra bits are discarded FOR BUS SELECTION, while the IOC READBACK RETURNS THE STORED VALUE.*  So masking the store would not merely be an unlicensed addition -- it would CHANGE THE READBACK POLICY, a guest-visible behaviour change.  My wording (\"extra bits are already discarded at the only consumer\") is true of bus selection and false of readback.\n\n*** Q3 -- THE WARRANT IS CHALLENGED, AND THIS IS THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND. ***  The seat volunteered a COUNTEREXAMPLE under its \"something else relevant\" clause: `dev_sh4.c\u0027s RCR1 arm carries a comment that explicitly discusses its FORMER store-before-exit ordering and the decision to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD, and the current code confirms that rejection.  In the seat\u0027s words: **\"a concrete counterexample to treating \u0027the store already ran\u0027 as a general warrant for acceptance.\"**  The fork this opens is real and must be answered rather than glossed:\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1), KEEP the store that already ran   (the proposal as briefed)\n    B  DECLINE the write -- move the store below the guard, as SH4 RCR1 deliberately did\nand the W_T_BASE0/W_T_BASE1 siblings do not settle it, because accepting-anything there may be absence of a guard rather than a positive decision that accepting is correct.  The seat says so directly.  NOT resolved here; carried as the round\u0027s open design question.\n\n**Q4/Q5 -- THE DISPOSITION SET IS INCOMPLETE, TWO MORE WITH SOURCE EXAMPLES.**  (i) CONTINUE WITH REPEATED ANNOUNCEMENTS: `dev_sh4.c\u0027 RCR2 stores first then calls debug() per matching TODO condition (ACCEPTS); `dev_pcc2.c\u0027 byte writes to PSCALEADJ are IGNORED with an unlatched fatal() -- so accept-and-announce and decline-and-announce both exist, unlatched, in shipped code.  (ii) COMPLETE WITH A GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR: `dev_wdc.c\u0027 wdc_command() sets WDCE_ABRT and requests an interrupt for EVERY unsupported command, independently of its once-only diagnostic; DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) exposes the error register and DEVICE_TICK(wdc) asserts the interrupt.  That is a disposition none of the carrier\u0027s SEVEN members names in this form -- the guest is told, in the architected way, and execution continues.  The seat is careful that these are existing alternatives, NOT evidence LCA should acquire either.  Also: `dev_pcc2.c\u0027 VECBASE MASKS the stored value and diagnoses the excluded bits -- the mask-and-announce shape, in the tree.\n\n**Q6 CONFIRMED, PLUS A CORRECTION TO MY OWN WORDING.**  The `default:\u0027 arm still diagnoses and exits FOR BOTH READS AND WRITES, and neither LCA_IOC_STAT0 nor LCA_IOC_STAT1 has a case at all.  Additional explicit exits remain in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf), DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa), and lca_interrupt_assert()/lca_interrupt_deassert().  **THE CORRECTION: I wrote that the default arm covers \"registers the header documents as read/write-1-to-clear\" -- the header marks TWO BITS IN STAT0 (IOC_STAT0_ERR, IOC_STAT0_LOST) as R/W1C and does NOT label STAT1 that way.**  So: the six-arm change can be COMPLETE while firmware can still terminate the emulator, which the round must say out loud rather than claim the register file is safe.\n\n*The interrupt-assert/deassert pair are bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)\u0027 with NO GUARD -- which is the carrier\u0027s MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT) case, added 2026-09-06 from this very file: there is no value being refused and nothing to decline, accept or fault.  And the seat re-confirmed from core/debugmsg.c that `fatal()\u0027 itself does NOT terminate -- the explicit exit(1) is what does.*"},{"seat":"grok","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"fork-mis-posed","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907","note":"pass-1 design panel on the A/B fork.  VERDICT: A for five; CONF is different and is NOT B"},{"seat":"glm","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"fork-mis-posed","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907","note":"pass-1 design panel on the A/B fork.  VERDICT: A for five (as accept+announce-once); CONF -\u003e B"},{"seat":"minimax","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"fork-mis-posed","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907","note":"pass-1 design panel on the A/B fork.  VERDICT: A for all six; CONF is MORE clearly A, and B can break its consumer"},{"seat":"kimi","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"fork-mis-posed","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907","note":"TRANSPORT: kimi-k3:cloud (the SUBSTITUTE -- the kimi CLI was 403-walled on its weekly limit, so this seat is COVERED, not lost; the substitute is PACKET-FED, so this answer is attack reasoning and NOT a repo-fact reading).  pass-1 design panel on the A/B fork.  VERDICT: split per arm: CONF decline-or-mask; five get drop-exit-plus-announce"},{"seat":"deepseek","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"fork-mis-posed","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907","note":"pass-1 design panel on the A/B fork.  VERDICT: DISSENT: premise 6 unsound, B for all six"},{"seat":"codex","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"REFUTED-PREMISE","outcome":"recommends-B","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/codex.txt","note":"pass-1 design panel.  VERDICT: \"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.  I recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior remains unproved.\"  VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY -- see the state_note.  This overturns the panel majority on a checkable fact."},{"seat":"fable5","phase":"design","d":"HELD","date":"2026-09-07","grade":"HELD","outcome":"","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/","hold_question":"Given that `rtc_rcr1` is MEASURED to have zero functional consumers (four occurrences: declaration, readback, store, and one inside the comment), does #447\u0027s precedent now support option B for all six arms -- and is B even well-defined at reset, when W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 reset to 0, a value not in their own accepted sets?","note":"[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The design stage ran SIX seats and no flagship seat, by standing owner directive reserving opus5 and fable5 entirely for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  This is a DELIBERATE hold, not a seat failure and not agreement.  QUESTION: Given that `rtc_rcr1` is MEASURED to have zero functional consumers (four occurrences: declaration, readback, store, and one inside the comment), does #447\u0027s precedent now support option B for all six arms -- and is B even well-defined at reset, when W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 reset to 0, a value not in their own accepted sets?  Queued as fable_queue.md section 7i."},{"seat":"codex","phase":"design","d":"�","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"THIRD-MEMBER-C:decline-and-preserve","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition2_20260908.txt","note":"VERDICT: **C -- decline out-of-set writes, PRESERVE the previous register value, report once.**  *** IT DISSOLVED THE ROW\u0027S TWO-OPTION FRAMING BY SHOWING A AND B ARE THE SAME OPTION, CORRECTED WHAT #447 ACTUALLY DOES, AND FOUND A CONSUMER THE ROW SAID DID NOT EXIST. ***  The census survives its audit."},{"seat":"codex","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"CONFIRMS 12/13 per register -- and adds alignment, a 2-bit CONF field, and the manual own required init that W_BASE1 rejects","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  MANUAL_brief, 8,657 b, file-reading.  Read S6.4 for all six registers and gave per-register denominators."},{"seat":"grok","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"answered, 14,818 b","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  MANUAL_brief, file-reading."},{"seat":"glm","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"the S3 arithmetic is right -- re-derived every step, no defect","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  PACKET_brief."},{"seat":"kimi","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"your derivation is right: shift, both fields, the thirteen-count and the invariant all check out","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  PACKET_brief (kimi-k3:cloud substitute; the CLI is 403-walled)."},{"seat":"minimax","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"the S3 derivation is correct","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  PACKET_brief."},{"seat":"deepseek","phase":"assess","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"CORRECT *** ONLY IF *** bits \u003c19:0\u003e must be zero -- and that premise is NOT established by my quote","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_20260908/","note":"ASSESS PANEL.  PACKET_brief.  THE ONLY SEAT TO CHALLENGE THE PREMISE, and it was right."},{"seat":"codex","phase":"design","d":"y","date":"2026-09-08","grade":"ANSWERED","outcome":"preserve masked state; validate the W_MASK FIELD; remove the W_BASE magic-value whitelists; TB_ENA still needs its reserved-bit guard corrected","grader":"main-loop","ev":"regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_fixdesign_20260908.txt","note":"FIX DESIGN, 9,504 b, file-reading.  Qualified three of the assess panel findings, including one of mine that had already been recorded."},{"seat":"fable5","phase":"assess","d":"2026-09-09","date":"2026-09-09","note":"ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork\u0027s guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design\u0027s \u0027is B well-defined at reset\u0027 MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule\u0027s own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork\u0027s own comment says \u0027only bit 0 is implemented so far\u0027, an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat\u0027s \u0027not a functional defect\u0027 OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs \u003e len\u0027 termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.","grader":"main-loop (Fable 5.1)","grade":"RULING","ev":"_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9586-9598,10791-10792"},{"seat":"fable5","phase":"research","d":"2026-09-09","date":"2026-09-09","note":"ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork\u0027s guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design\u0027s \u0027is B well-defined at reset\u0027 MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule\u0027s own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork\u0027s own comment says \u0027only bit 0 is implemented so far\u0027, an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat\u0027s \u0027not a functional defect\u0027 OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs \u003e len\u0027 termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.","grader":"main-loop (Fable 5.1)","grade":"RULING","ev":"_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9586-9598,10791-10792"},{"seat":"fable5","phase":"design","d":"2026-09-09","date":"2026-09-09","note":"ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  RULING (all three held stages, one ruling): the round is to WIDEN THE WHITELISTS, not merely to change what happens when they fail -- the guards are WRONG, not harsh.  Warrant is the LCA manual, LOCAL since 2026-09-08 (_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt) and read rather than cited from a seat: Table 6-6 (:9586-9598) enumerates THIRTEEN legal W_MASK values and the fork accepts ONE; S6.4.14 item 3 (:10791-10792) REQUIRES SROM to write ZERO to both W_BASE registers, which the fork\u0027s guard REJECTS.  So decline-and-preserve is NOT defensible as modelling for those four arms -- the honest model ACCEPTS the documented set -- which answers research (a) and (b) together, and makes design\u0027s \u0027is B well-defined at reset\u0027 MOOT: once 0 is in the accepted set, as the manual requires, there is no reset conflict.  SCOPE: ONE round (one file, one machine, one semantic class, one detector -- the reopening rule\u0027s own test), FOUR arms: W_MASK0/1 and W_BASE0/1.  CONF is option-set member 4, ACCEPT: the fork\u0027s own comment says \u0027only bit 0 is implemented so far\u0027, an implementation limit, not an illegal value.  TB_ENA is ASSESSED OUT of this round: both TEN states are accepted today; the morning seat\u0027s \u0027not a functional defect\u0027 OVER-CLEARED an ignored-bit nuance, which is DISCLOSED here rather than fixed.  The `ofs \u003e len\u0027 termination is DOUBLE-COUNTED with lcaisalane and is assigned there.  Nuance owed in the CHANGELOG (codex): the thirteen are canonical field settings, not thirteen drop-in substitutions with bases held fixed.  DISCHARGED.","grader":"main-loop (Fable 5.1)","grade":"RULING","ev":"_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9586-9598,10791-10792"}]
